, and am no longer (merely) about to be led away: cp.
O. C. 1074 ἔρδουσ’ ἢ μέλλουσιν;
Ph. 1255 κἀμέ τοι | ταὐτὸν τόδ’ ὄψει δρῶντα κοὐ μέλλοντ’ ἔτι. Meineke's conjecture μελλώ (=μέλλησις, Aesch. Ag. 1356) was needless.— Seyffert understands, ‘I make no more delay,’—said firmly and proudly: she scorns to bring punishment on her guards by detaining them longer. But this does not suit ἄγομαι δή.

, acc. of respect; 

									Od. 15.267
